## Final Judgement
The fact-check consistently found no evidence to support the claim. Multiple searches, including reviews of official party platforms, statements from prominent Democratic officials (such as the President and Speaker of the House), proposed federal legislation, and specific actions taken during the COVID-19 pandemic, revealed no instances where Democrats called for the permanent closure of churches. Instead, evidence shows that any restrictions were temporary public health measures applied broadly during the pandemic, with churches sometimes receiving exemptions. Prominent fact-checking organizations like Politifact explicitly concluded there was no evidence for the claim, rating it "Pants on Fire."

The evidence from the record is comprehensive and clear, covering all relevant angles—from official party positions to statements by leaders and actions at both federal and local levels. No further investigation is required to address the claim.

Based on the recorded evidence, which consistently shows no official Democratic policy, no statements from leadership, no proposed legislation, and no specific actions advocating for the permanent shutdown of U.S. churches, the claim that "The Democrats want to shut down U.S. churches permanently" is explicitly and clearly refuted. The conclusion is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
No evidence supports the claim that Democrats seek to permanently close U.S. churches. [PolitiFact](https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2020/oct/08/donald-trump/trump-falsely-claims-democrats-want-shut-your-chur/) found no statements from Democratic leaders, no official party platform planks, and no proposed legislation advocating for such a goal, rating the claim "Pants on Fire." The [Statesman](https://www.statesman.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/06/24/fact-check-does-joe-biden-want-to-prosecute-churchgoers/113957728/) confirmed that neither Joe Biden nor other leading Democrats made statements calling for permanent church closures or prosecution of churchgoers. Any restrictions during the COVID-19 pandemic were temporary public health measures applied broadly across gatherings, with churches sometimes receiving exemptions, as noted by both PolitiFact and [CBS News](https://www.cbsnews.com/pittsburgh/news/pennsylvania-house-governor-barred-shutting-down-churches/). Trump's assertion was based solely on a video of an anti-mask protest in Moscow, Idaho, and the White House provided no supporting evidence when contacted. The claim is refuted.